Emma (docs): Quick sync — I need to update the rate limits/quotas doc for the pricing tier migration PR. Question: what actually happens when a paid customer with burst credits hits a token spike? Do we: 1) consume burst credits and continue at normal speed, 2) consume credits then fall back to lower-tier throughput, or 3) immediately 429 and surface Retry-After? Need exact sequence to write a clear example. :thinking:

Zach (eng-runtime): Short answer: we consume burst credits first, then apply the tier rate. If both are exhausted the request gets 429. We also emit headers so clients can handle it gracefully.

Zach (eng-runtime): Sequence we should document: 1) Check available burst credits for the account. 2) If credits >= request cost, deduct and allow. 3) If credits < cost, consume remaining credits and attempt to serve remaining tokens under tier rate. 4) If effective throughput exceeds current rate window, reject with 429 and include Retry-After + X-Redwood-Quota header.

Priya (product): Helpful. For the docs example, can we include an SDK snippet that shows handling 429 + using Retry-After header to back off? Also want a short note in the user-facing pricing page about "burst credits smooth spikes for N tokens/month" — but the behavior nuance matters for SDK examples.

Zach (eng-runtime): Yup. Here's a compact example for both Python and JS to add to the migration guide. Use a small backoff + header parse and show a fallback to exponential backoff.

Zach (eng-runtime): ```python
# python example: handle 429 and use Retry-After
import time
import requests

url = "https://api.redwood.example/v1/generate"
headers = {"Authorization": "Bearer $REDWOOD_KEY"}
body = {"model": "open-llama-3b", "input": "..."}

for attempt in range(5):
    r = requests.post(url, json=body, headers=headers)
    if r.status_code == 200:
        print(r.json())
        break
    if r.status_code == 429:
        ra = r.headers.get("Retry-After") or r.headers.get("X-Redwood-Quota-Reset")
        wait = float(ra) if ra and ra.isnumeric() else (2 ** attempt) * 0.5
        print(f"429 received, sleeping {wait}s (attempt {attempt})")
        time.sleep(wait)
        continue
    r.raise_for_status()
```

Zach (eng-runtime): ```javascript
// JS fetch example: parse Retry-After and X-Redwood-Quota header
async function callWithBackoff(body) {
  const url = 'https://api.redwood.example/v1/generate';
  const headers = { 'Authorization': `Bearer ${process.env.REDWOOD_KEY}` };
  for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    const res = await fetch(url, { method: 'POST', headers, body: JSON.stringify(body) });
    if (res.status === 200) return await res.json();
    if (res.status === 429) {
      const ra = res.headers.get('Retry-After') || res.headers.get('X-Redwood-Quota-Reset');
      const wait = ra ? Number(ra) : Math.pow(2, i) * 500;
      console.log(`429 -> wait ${wait}ms`);
      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, wait));
      continue;
    }
    throw new Error(`unexpected status ${res.status}`);
  }
}
```

